        <div class="p-4 bg-canvas rounded-xl border border-hairline">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-base mb-2">Projected vs. Shallow</h3>
          <p class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Projected:</strong> Tissue extends outward from the ribcage like a cone or glass. Looks larger visually in profile; best suited for deep, unlined seamed balconette cups.<br/><br/>
            <strong>Shallow:</strong> Tissue is distributed broadly over a wide chest area. Appears smaller visually in clothing; best suited for demi, push-up, or wide-wire balconette styles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 bg-canvas rounded-xl border border-hairline">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-base mb-2">Full on Top vs. Full on Bottom</h3>
          <p class="leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Full on Top (FoT):</strong> Greater tissue mass above the nipple. Prone to cup spillage in plunge bras; excels in open-top half cups.<br/><br/>
            <strong>Full on Bottom (FoB):</strong> Greater tissue mass below the nipple. Prone to gaping at the upper edge in rigid foam bras; excels in plunge and teardrop cups.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

